\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Which symmetry selectors restrict}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let a finite group #{\Gamma} act orthogonally on a
finite-dimensional real Euclidean space #{H}, and let
#{Q>0} be self-adjoint and #{\Gamma}-equivariant. Two constructions
of \vocabk{symmetry selectors}{fdip-005A} have different criteria.}
\ol{
\li{For a self-adjoint idempotent #{e\in\mathbb R[\Gamma]},
the selector #{P=\rho(e)} restricts whenever its rank is nonzero
and proper. Its image need not be a #{\Gamma}-subrepresentation.}
\li{Let #{X} be a nonzero proper #{\Gamma}-subrepresentation.
For each irreducible #{W_\Xi}, write
#{M_\Xi=\operatorname{Hom}_\Gamma(W_\Xi,H)} and
#{M_\Xi(X)=\operatorname{Hom}_\Gamma(W_\Xi,X)}, with the right
#{D_\Xi}-action of the \vocabk{multiplicity module}{fdip-00KN}.
Postcomposition
##{(Q\cdot f)(w)=Q(f(w))}
is real-linear on #{M_\Xi} and self-adjoint for the pairing
#{h(f,g)=f^{\dagger}\circ g}. The selector restricts precisely when
##{Q\cdot M_\Xi(X)\subseteq M_\Xi(X)
 \qquad\text{for every }\Xi.}
Equivalently, each #{M_\Xi(X)} admits a right
#{D_\Xi}-basis of eigenvectors of #{Q\cdot}. Repeated eigenvalues
allow a proper subspace of an eigenspace; whole eigenspaces need
not be selected.}
}

\p{The right-scalar column form of this criterion is
\ref{fdip-00LO}.}

\proof{
\p{Equivariance makes #{Q} commute with every #{\rho(g)}, hence
with #{\rho(e)}. The [restriction criterion](fdip-005M) proves
the first assertion. Centrality is not needed for this commutation;
it would additionally make #{\rho(e)} commute with the group action.}

\p{For a subrepresentation #{X}, both #{X} and #{X^\perp} are
#{\Gamma}-stable, so its orthogonal projector belongs to the
commutant. By evaluation in that module and the
identification in \ref{fdip-004Z}, every vector of
#{X\cap H_\Xi} is a finite sum of values #{f(w)} with
#{f\in M_\Xi(X)} and #{w\in W_\Xi}. Thus #{QX\subseteq X} if and
only if #{Q\circ f} still lands in #{X} for every such #{f}.
Equivariance of #{Q} keeps #{Q\circ f} an intertwiner, so this is
the displayed invariance of #{M_\Xi(X)}. Summing over the isotypic
components gives the criterion on #{X}. Self-adjointness of #{Q}
preserves the orthogonal complement once it preserves #{X}. Apply
\ref{fdip-005M}.}

\p{The same self-adjointness gives
#{h(Q\cdot f,g)=h(f,Q\cdot g)}. On an irreducible, a nonzero
intertwiner satisfies #{f^{\dagger}\circ f=c\bbI} with #{c>0}, by
\ref{fdip-004Z}, so #{f\mapsto\operatorname{Re}h(f,f)} is a
positive definite real inner product and #{Q\cdot} is self-adjoint
for it. It is also right-linear. A real eigenvector therefore
generates a right #{D_\Xi}-line of eigenvectors. Right
multiplication by a unit scalar is orthogonal for this real
product, so the orthogonal complement of the line is a right
submodule and is invariant. Applied to an invariant
#{M_\Xi(X)}, induction gives a right #{D_\Xi}-orthonormal
eigenbasis. Conversely, a right span of eigenvectors is invariant.}
}

\p{In a multiplicity-free representation every
#{m_\Xi=1}, and a right #{D_\Xi}-submodule of the multiplicity
module is either zero or all of it. Thus every nonzero proper
subrepresentation restricts. At higher multiplicity, group
stability alone does not suffice, as the [prism line](fdip-005O)
shows.}
